Day 02: Kubernetes Pods, Labels and Selectors
Objective

Practice Kubernetes Pod management and understand how labels and selectors work using a KOPS-managed cluster on AWS.

Topics Covered
Verify Kubernetes cluster nodes
Create Pods using kubectl run
Create Pods using YAML manifests
Inspect Pods using kubectl describe
List Pods and view their labels
Add, update, and remove labels
Filter Pods using label selectors
Delete Pods
Clean up the KOPS cluster after practice
1. Verify the Cluster
kubectl get nodes
kubectl get node
kubectl get no

2. Create a Pod
kubectl run pod-1 --image=nginx
kubectl get po

3. Create a Pod Using YAML

Create pod.yml:

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: pod-2
  labels:
    app: swiggy
    env: dev
spec:
  containers:
    - name: app-cont
      image: shaikmustafa/dm
      ports:
        - containerPort: 80


Create or update the Pod:

kubectl apply -f pod.yml
kubectl get po
kubectl get po -o wide
kubectl describe pod pod-2

4. View Pod Labels
kubectl get po --show-labels

5. Add and Update Labels
kubectl label pod pod-2 client=flm
kubectl label pod pod-2 app=zomato --overwrite
kubectl get po --show-labels


Restore the original app label if required:

kubectl label pod pod-2 app=swiggy --overwrite

6. Filter Pods Using Selectors
kubectl get po -l app=swiggy
kubectl get po -l env=test
kubectl get po -l client=flm
kubectl get po -l 'client!=flm'


Match multiple possible values:

kubectl get po -l 'app in (swiggy,zomato)'
kubectl get po -l 'app in (swiggy)'
kubectl get po -l 'app in (zomato)'


Match multiple requirements:

kubectl get po -l 'app=swiggy,env=dev'

7. Remove Labels
kubectl label pod pod-2 client-
kubectl get po --show-labels


Remove multiple labels from pod-1 if they exist:

kubectl label pod pod-1 app- client- env-

8. Delete a Pod
kubectl delete pod pod-1
kubectl get po

9. Delete the KOPS Cluster After Practice

Verify the state store and cluster name before deleting:

export KOPS_STATE_STORE=s3://mustafa-kops777
kops get cluster
kops delete cluster --name rajesh.k8s.local --yes


Warning: Cluster deletion is destructive and may remove AWS resources. Confirm the cluster name and account before proceeding.

Learning Outcome

Today I practiced Pod creation and inspection, YAML manifests, label management, label selectors, and Kubernetes cluster cleanup.

